From 6b8a58d285d2820d210dab71835eaf82b4291129 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Chris <chris.dixon.78.burnbridge@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 21 Aug 2026 12:21:10 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] GS: request the two ESSL extensions the shaders actually
 need

The port loaded, negotiated an ES 3.2 context and ran the emulator -- it read
the disc and identified the game -- but drew a solid white frame. Nothing in
GSShaderOGL checks GL_COMPILE_STATUS or GL_LINK_STATUS, so two shaders were
being rejected by the driver in complete silence and the renderer carried on
with programs that were never built.

glslangValidator had passed all seven stages, which is why this was missed:
it is more permissive than Mesa's ESSL front end. Compiling the same sources
through glCompileShader on a real ES 3.2 context reports what it would not:

  tfx.glsl      index layout qualifier requires EXT_blend_func_extended
  tfx_vgs.glsl  redeclaration of gl_PerVertex must be a subset of the
                built-in members of gl_PerVertex

Both are things desktop GLSL 330 simply has. Dual-source blending uses
layout(index = 1), which ESSL puts behind EXT_blend_func_extended. And
gl_PointSize is not a member of gl_PerVertex on ES unless
EXT_geometry_point_size says it is, while the common header redeclares the
block with it in for the geometry stage.

Both extensions are requested per stage, and they go ahead of the precision
declarations because #extension has to precede every non-preprocessor token.

With this, SSX boots and renders through the ES path.
---
 pcsx2/GS/Renderers/OpenGL/GSShaderOGL.cpp | 18 ++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 18 insertions(+)

diff --git a/pcsx2/GS/Renderers/OpenGL/GSShaderOGL.cpp b/pcsx2/GS/Renderers/OpenGL/GSShaderOGL.cpp
index bdf576260..4b67f8a8d 100644
--- a/pcsx2/GS/Renderers/OpenGL/GSShaderOGL.cpp
+++ b/pcsx2/GS/Renderers/OpenGL/GSShaderOGL.cpp
@@ -254,6 +254,24 @@ std::string GSShaderOGL::GenGlslHeader(const std::string& entry, GLenum type, co
 	// and image type used anywhere in these shaders needs one declared or the
 	// compiler stops at the declaration.
 	header = "#version 320 es\n";
+
+	// #extension has to precede every non-preprocessor token, so it goes ahead
+	// of the precision declarations below rather than next to the stage
+	// defines. Both of these are things desktop GLSL 330 simply has:
+	//
+	//  - the fragment shader uses layout(index = 1) for dual-source blending,
+	//    which ESSL puts behind EXT_blend_func_extended;
+	//  - gl_PointSize is not a member of gl_PerVertex on ES unless
+	//    EXT_geometry_point_size says so, and the common header redeclares the
+	//    block with it in, so the geometry shader is rejected without this.
+	//
+	// Both are silent failures otherwise: nothing here checks
+	// GL_COMPILE_STATUS, so a rejected shader just renders white.
+	if (type == GL_FRAGMENT_SHADER)
+		header += "#extension GL_EXT_blend_func_extended : require\n";
+	if (type == GL_GEOMETRY_SHADER)
+		header += "#extension GL_EXT_geometry_point_size : require\n";
+
 	header += "precision highp float;\n";
 	header += "precision highp int;\n";
 	header += "precision highp sampler2D;\n";
